The pair $(M, G)$ is a \emph{tame corank-one degeneration} if:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(T1)] $\Sig$ is a smooth closed hypersurface of $M$;
\item[(T2)] $G$ has corank exactly one at every point of $\Sig$, and the radical lines assemble into a smooth line subbundle $\shL \subset T^*M|_\Sig$, locally spanned by a covector field $k$;
\item[(T3)] (transversality) the degeneration is first-order: the canonical section $\delta$ of Lemma \claimref{sheaf.invariant-form-t3} below is nowhere vanishing on $\Sig$.
\end{enumerate}

Let $n$ be odd and let $\Cl^{0}$ be the even subalgebra of $\Cl(p,q)$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] If $\omega^{2} = +1$, then $\Cl(p,q) \cong \Cl^{0} \oplus \Cl^{0}$, the two summands the ideals of the complementary central idempotents $(1 \pm \omega)/2$, and on the summand of $(1 \pm \omega)/2$ the generator $e_{n}$ acts as $\pm s\,\omega'$, with $s = e_{n}^{2}$ and $\omega' = e_{1}\cdots e_{n-1}$ the volume element of the shadow (so the two summands carry opposite signs).
\item[(ii)] If $\omega^{2} = -1$, then $\Cl(p,q) \cong \Cl^{0} \otimes_{\R} \C$ with $\omega$ as the imaginary unit.
\item[(iii)] $\Cl^{0}(p, q+1) \cong \Cl(p,q)$ and $\Cl^{0}(p+1, q) \cong \Cl(q,p)$.
\end{enumerate}

In $\Cl(2,3)$, the time extension of this world with $e_{5}^{2} = +1$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] the generators $e_{1}, \ldots, e_{4}$ generate a copy of $\Cl(1,3)$, this world's shadow;
\item[(ii)] the bivectors $e_{\mu}e_{5}$, $\mu = 1, \ldots, 4$, anticommute pairwise, square to $(-1, +1, +1, +1)$, and generate the whole even subalgebra, which is a copy of $\Cl(3,1)$, the mirror's shadow;
\item[(iii)] the two copies meet exactly in the even part of the shadow, $\Cl^{0}(1,3) \cong \Mat(2,\C)$, of dimension eight, the Lorentz algebra with its Weyl spinors; and together with $\omega$ they span everything, $\Cl(2,3) = \Cl(1,3) \oplus \omega\,\Cl(1,3)$;
\item[(iv)] no automorphism of $\Cl(2,3)$ carries the copy of $\Cl(1,3)$ onto the copy of $\Cl(3,1)$; the natural map between their generating sets is multiplication by the central $\omega$, $e_{\mu} \mapsto \omega e_{\mu}$, which squares to $-e_{\mu}^{2}$ (the Wick rotation) and is complex-linear, not an automorphism of the real algebra.
\end{enumerate}
In $\Cl(1,4)$, the space extension with $e_{5}^{2} = -1$: the generators $e_{\mu}$ and the bivectors $e_{\mu}e_{5}$ both generate copies of $\Cl(1,3)$, the elements $\omega e_{\mu}$ generate a third, and $\Cl(3,1)$ admits no unital embedding into $\Cl(1,4)$ at all. The same statements hold for the mirror's two extensions with the roles of $\Cl(1,3)$ and $\Cl(3,1)$ exchanged.

Under Fact \claimref{odd-extensions.imported-frozen-point}, write $\Cl(I^{\perp})$ for the Clifford bundle of the rank-five bundle $I^{\perp}$, and let $e_{5} = V/|V|$ be the unit Stelle-West direction, so that the Lorentz frame $e_{1}, \ldots, e_{4}$ of $\{I,V\}^{\perp}$ and $e_{5}$ together generate $\Cl(I^{\perp})$ fibrewise. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] for $\Lambda > 0$, $\Cl(I^{\perp}) \cong \Cl(1,4)$, $e_{5}^{2} = -1$, and the translation bivectors $e_{\mu}e_{5}$ of the MacDowell-Mansouri connection generate this world's shadow $\Cl(1,3)$;
\item[(ii)] for $\Lambda < 0$, $\Cl(I^{\perp}) \cong \Cl(2,3)$, $e_{5}^{2} = +1$, and the translation bivectors generate a copy of the mirror's abstract algebra $\Cl(3,1)$, while this world's shadow is the subalgebra generated by the Lorentz frame (what this copy is and is not: Proposition \claimref{odd-extensions.belongs-tower} and Corollary \claimref{odd-extensions.two-readings-which-which}).
\end{enumerate}
In either case the fibrewise algebra $\Cl(I^{\perp})$ is one of the two extensions of Corollary \claimref{odd-extensions.four-extensions-oriented-pair}, and the Poincar\'e contraction $\ell \to \infty$ leaves the Clifford type of the translation algebra unchanged.

Let $\Cl(2,4)$ be the ambient of this world's conformal completion (Fact \claimref{odd-extensions.imported-conformal-completion}). Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] $\Cl^{0}(2,4) \cong \Cl(2,3) \cong \Cl(4,1) \cong \Mat(4,\C)$, the two odd algebras being generated inside the even subalgebra by $e_{i}e_{6}$ for a spacelike $e_{6}$ and by $e_{i}e_{1}$ for a timelike $e_{1}$ respectively; the rung's volume element $\omega_{6}$ (the conformal pseudoscalar, $I_6$ up to sign) is even, squares to $-1$ and commutes with every even element, and is the complex unit of $\Mat(4,\C)$;
\item[(ii)] inside $\Cl^{0}(2,4)$ both oriented shadows occur: the bivectors $e_{i}e_{6}$ for $i = 2, \ldots, 5$ generate a $\Cl(1,3)$ and the bivectors $e_{i}e_{1}$ for $i = 2, \ldots, 5$ generate a $\Cl(3,1)$;
\item[(iii)] every input to the copy theorem (Fact \claimref{odd-extensions.imported-copy-theorem-soldered-residue}) is an object of $\Cl^{0}(2,4)$ or of its complexified modules, hence of the complexification in which $\Cl(1,3)$ and $\Cl(3,1)$ are two real forms of one algebra;
\item[(iv)] signature reversal is not an isomorphism of the odd extensions: $\Cl(2,3) \not\cong \Cl(3,2)$ and $\Cl(1,4) \not\cong \Cl(4,1)$, the centre being $\C$ in $\Cl(2,3)$ and $\Cl(4,1)$ and $\R \oplus \R$ in $\Cl(3,2)$ and $\Cl(1,4)$.
\end{enumerate}

In the tractor bundle of signature $(2,4)$ let $I$ be the frozen point, $Q(I) = \Lambda/3$, and for $\Lambda \neq 0$ let $V$ be the Stelle-West vector. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] $I$ and $V$ span a hyperbolic plane: $\ip{I}{V} = 0$, $Q(I)$ and $Q(V)$ have opposite signs, and the unit directions $e_{I}$, $e_{V}$ anticommute with each other and with the Lorentz frame of $\{I,V\}^{\perp}$;
\item[(ii)] $I^{\perp}$ is spanned by the Lorentz frame and $e_{V}$, and $\Cl(I^{\perp})$ is the subalgebra of the rung generated by these five: the space extension $\Cl(1,4)$ for $\Lambda > 0$ ($e_{V}^{2} = -1$) and the time extension $\Cl(2,3)$ for $\Lambda < 0$ ($e_{V}^{2} = +1$); adjoining $e_{I}$ returns the whole rung, so the ascent factors as
\[
\Cl(1,3) \;\subset\; \Cl(I^{\perp}) \;\subset\; \Cl(2,4),
\]
with the sign of $\Lambda$ deciding which of the pair's two signed directions is adjoined first;
\item[(iii)] at $\Lambda = 0$ the frozen point, written $I_{0}$ from here on, is null and lies in its own complement, $I^{\perp}$ is degenerate of signature $(1,3;1)$, and $\Cl(I^{\perp}) \cong \Cl(1,3;1)$ is the degenerate algebra with $I$ itself the nilpotent generator: $I^{2} = 0$, $I$ anticommutes with the Lorentz frame, and $I\,\Cl(1,3;1)$ is a sixteen-dimensional ideal of square zero;
\item[(iv)] the Lorentz frame $\{I,V\}^{\perp}$ has signature $(1,3)$ for every value of $\Lambda$, including zero.
\end{enumerate}

Under the dark-matter paper's Theorem 3.5 the residue of a sector is the signature of the soldered vector space, the image of the tangent space in the algebra with $v^{2} = g(v,v)$ (Fact \claimref{odd-extensions.imported-copy-theorem-soldered-residue}). For the algebra $\Cl(I^{\perp})$ of Theorem \claimref{odd-extensions.are-ascents-pair-signed}:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] the soldered vector space is the Lorentz frame $\{I,V\}^{\perp} = e(TM)$, the image of the soldering form, of signature $(1,3)$ for every $\Lambda$ (Theorem \claimref{odd-extensions.are-ascents-pair-signed}(iv)); $V$ is not in it, being orthogonal to $e(TM)$ with $\nabla V = e$ (Fact \claimref{odd-extensions.imported-frozen-point});
\item[(ii)] hence $\Cl(I^{\perp})$ has tower charge $6$: it is a sub-object of this world's rung, the intermediate algebra of this world's own ascent, and not the ground shadow of the residue-$5$ or residue-$7$ tower whose abstract algebra it shares;
\item[(iii)] the coincidence $\Cl(I^{\perp}) \cong \Cl(1,4)$ for $\Lambda > 0$ is induced by an isometry of quadratic spaces ($I^{\perp}$ has signature $(1,4)$ outright) but by no map of soldered data: the isometry carries $e_{V}$, which is not soldered, to a fifth tangent direction of the five-dimensional shadow, which is, and carries the four-dimensional soldered space into a proper subspace of that shadow's tangent space.
\end{enumerate}

Let $W = I_{0}^{\perp}$, the degenerate space of signature $(1,3;1)$ at $\Lambda = 0$ with radical spanned by the null frozen point $I_{0}$, written $I$ in this theorem, and let $\widetilde W$ be its minimal completion. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] $\widetilde W$ has signature $(2,4)$ and is the rung; the isometry of Fact \claimref{odd-extensions.imported-minimal-completion-degenerate-algebra-rule} fixing $W$ pointwise is unique, so the null partner $\varphi$ of $I$ with $Q(I,\varphi) = 1$, $Q(\varphi,\varphi) = 0$, $\varphi \perp \{I\}^{\perp} \cap W$ is canonical: the transport supplies the hyperbolic plane $\mathrm{span}(I, \varphi)$ together with a distinguished null basis of it;
\item[(ii)] every vector orthogonal to the Lorentz frame is $aI + b\varphi$ with norm $2ab$; a frozen point of cosmological constant $\Lambda$ is such a vector with $2ab = \Lambda/3$, and the sign of $\Lambda$ is the sign of the product of its components along the null generator and its canonical partner;
\item[(iii)] the isometries of the completion that fix the Lorentz frame act on the plane as $O(1,1)$, whose orbits on nonzero vectors are exactly the level sets of the norm; so the only invariant of the frozen point under the symmetries the transport supplies is its norm $\Lambda/3$: the cosmological constant, sign and magnitude, is the one modulus of the frozen point that the transport leaves free;
\item[(iv)] no symmetry of the completion carries a frozen point of positive norm to one of negative norm, and the two intermediate algebras $\Cl(I_{\Lambda}^{\perp})$ for the two signs are non-isomorphic (Theorem \claimref{odd-extensions.twistor-algebra}(iv)); the two signs are inequivalent under every symmetry the construction has, and the choice between them is decided by nothing in the transport.
\end{enumerate}

In the rung, with the canonical null frame realised as $I = (e_{+} + e_{-})/2$, $\varphi = e_{+} - e_{-}$ in the hyperbolic plane orthogonal to the Lorentz frame:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] the bivector $B = I \wedge \varphi = -e_{+}e_{-}$ squares to $+1$, commutes with every Lorentz bivector and with every Lorentz vector, and anticommutes with $e_{\pm}$: it is the generator of the dilations of the conformal model;
\item[(ii)] the rotor $R = \cosh\theta + B\sinh\theta$ fixes the Lorentz frame and acts on the plane by $I \mapsto tI$, $\varphi \mapsto \varphi/t$ with $t = e^{2\theta}$, so the boosts of Theorem \claimref{odd-extensions.transport-constrains-frozen-point-only-through}(iii) are the dilations;
\item[(iii)] hence the ratio $b/a$ of the frozen point's components is a dilation gauge and only the product $2ab = \Lambda/3$ is invariant, and by Fact \claimref{dark-energy.point-infinity-has-no-physical-fluctuation} the gauge is not fixed by the dynamics either: the components are unphysical, not merely symmetry-related, and the frame the transport supplies carries no quantity beyond $\Lambda$ itself.
\end{enumerate}

Both oriented towers are soldered to the one gravitational field $(\hb, \Omega)$ on flat spacetime (Definition \claimref{obj.synthesis.gtg-flat-spacetime-gauge-fields}), the mirror reading its derived metric with the sign reversed, $-g$, so the mirror's tractor bundle has signature $(4,2)$ (this world's tractors being the vectors of $\Cl(2,4)$ carried by the tractor gauge field of Theorem \claimref{synthesis.gtg-tractor-gauge-field-reproduces-tractor-connection}, the mirror's those of $\Cl(4,2)$) and its frozen point has norm $-Q(I)$. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] in a de Sitter universe the intermediate algebra of this world's ascent is the doubled $\Cl(1,4)$ and the mirror's is the complexified $\Cl(4,1)$; in an anti-de Sitter universe they are the complexified $\Cl(2,3)$ and the doubled $\Cl(3,2)$. The rule is: doubled if and only if (de Sitter and this world) or (anti-de Sitter and mirror);
\item[(ii)] the even subalgebras $\Cl^{0}(1,4)$ and $\Cl^{0}(4,1)$ have the same presentation, the bivectors $e_{\mu}e_{5}$ anticommuting with squares $(+,-,-,-)$, so the two towers' conformal-to-de-Sitter spin groups, spinor modules and MacDowell-Mansouri bivector algebras coincide; the derived content of the dark-matter paper's copy theorem, which runs through the spin group, does not see the doubling;
\item[(iii)] what differs is the bare fifth generator alone: $e_{5}^{2} = -1$ with $e_{5} = \pm\omega'$ on the two summands in the doubled case, against $e_{5}^{2} = +1$ with $e_{5} = \omega'^{-1}\omega$, a central complex unit times the pseudoscalar, in the complexified case.
\end{enumerate}
Consequently the sign of $\Lambda$ does not select the visible tower: it distinguishes the two towers' intermediate algebras, but through an element no derived quantity of the series uses, and that element is the unit vector along $V$, which Proposition \claimref{odd-extensions.belongs-tower} places outside the soldered space and Fact \claimref{dark-energy.point-infinity-has-no-physical-fluctuation} places in the pure-gauge sector.

Fix the dilation gauge by a smooth nowhere-vanishing $a(\Lambda)$ and write the frozen point in the canonical null frame as $I_{\Lambda} = a I_{0} + b\varphi$ with $2ab = \Lambda/3$ (Theorem \claimref{odd-extensions.transport-constrains-frozen-point-only-through}). Let $w_{\Lambda} = aI_{0} - b\varphi$, so that $I_{\Lambda}^{\perp}$ is spanned by the Lorentz frame and $w_{\Lambda}$. Then the family $(I_{\Lambda}^{\perp}, \text{induced form})$ over the $\Lambda$ line is a tame corank-one degeneration in the sense of Fact \claimref{odd-extensions.imported-tame-walls-exceptional-algebra-residue}:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] its Gram matrix in the frame $(e_{1}, \ldots, e_{4}, w_{\Lambda})$ is $\operatorname{diag}(1,-1,-1,-1,-\Lambda/3)$ for every $\Lambda$ and every gauge, so the degenerate locus is the point $\Lambda = 0$ (T1), the radical there is the line spanned by $I_{0}$ (T2), and the dying eigenvalue $-\Lambda/3$ crosses zero with nonzero speed (T3), $\delta(w_{0} \otimes w_{0}) = -\tfrac13\,d\Lambda$ in the parametrisation by $\Lambda$, the $3$ being that of $Q(I) = \Lambda/3$;
\item[(ii)] the signatures on the two sides are $(2,3)$ for $\Lambda < 0$ and $(1,4)$ for $\Lambda > 0$: the wall separates the anti-de Sitter and de Sitter intermediate algebras, and crossing it changes the sign of one direction's square, which is the sheaf paper's signature-change wall in a parameter rather than in spacetime;
\item[(iii)] the gauge $a = b$, which makes $I_{\Lambda}$ proportional to $I_{0} + \varphi$, is not smooth at the wall and is not defined for $\Lambda < 0$; the tame structure needs a smooth gauge, and the scalar invariants (the dying eigenvalue, its crossing speed, the signatures) do not depend on which, while the identification of the radical vector with $I_{0}$ at a fixed normalisation does, $w_{0} = a(0)\,I_{0}$.
\end{enumerate}

For the tame wall of Proposition \claimref{odd-extensions.line-tame-wall}:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] the sheaf paper's rescaling of the dying direction, $w_{\Lambda} \mapsto w_{\Lambda}/\sqrt{|\Lambda|}$, is proportional, by the fixed factor $\sqrt 3$, to the dark-energy paper's normalisation of the Stelle-West vector to unit length, $e_{V} = V/|V|$, both being normalisations of the same line; the exceptional algebra at the wall is the Lorentz frame orthogonal to the rate of degeneration, $\Cl(1,3) \perp \langle +\tfrac13\rangle \cong \Cl(2,3)$ approached from $\Lambda < 0$ and $\Cl(1,3) \perp \langle -\tfrac13\rangle \cong \Cl(1,4)$ from $\Lambda > 0$, the cross terms vanishing identically so that the rescaled family is exactly, not asymptotically, block diagonal;
\item[(ii)] the residue of the inverse form at the wall is $-3\,w_{0} \otimes w_{0}$, the coefficient fixed by the normalisation $Q(w_{\Lambda}) = -\Lambda/3$: the pole is the Stelle-West norm, $\Lambda\,Q(V) = -3$, one algebraic step from the imported identity of Fact \claimref{odd-extensions.imported-frozen-point-degenerate-algebras}; the sheaf paper's identification $N^{*}\Sigma \cong L^{\otimes 2}$ reads $d\Lambda \cong -3\, w_{0} \otimes w_{0}$, which is $-3\,I_{0} \otimes I_{0}$ when $a(0)^{2} = 1$, so the dying direction carries the weight of $|\Lambda|^{1/2}$ and the weightless normalisation of the frozen point's complement is by $\sqrt{|\Lambda|/3}$, which for $\Lambda > 0$ is the asymptotic de Sitter rate $H = \sqrt{\Lambda/3}$, the series' $H^{2} = Q(I)$;
\item[(iii)] the rung, with its tractor metric of constant signature $(2,4)$, is a nondegenerate completion of the family on both sides of the wall, of rank one more, the completing direction at the wall being the partner $\varphi$ and away from it the frozen point $I_{\Lambda}$ itself; this is not particular to the cosmological constant: for any $(p,q)$ the family of hyperplanes $\mathrm{span}(f_{1}, \ldots, f_{n}, u + \tfrac{t}{2}v)$ of $\R^{p,q} \perp H$, $H$ hyperbolic with null basis $u, v$, $\ip{u}{v} = 1$, is a tame corank-one family with dying eigenvalue $t$ completed on both sides by the fixed $(p+1,q+1)$, and the $\Lambda$ family is this model with $(p,q) = (1,3)$, $u = I_{0}$, $v = \varphi$, $t = -\Lambda/3$;
\item[(iv)] this does not contradict the conservation of degeneracy, and lies in the case its proof excludes by hypothesis: the complement of $I_{\Lambda}^{\perp}$ in the rung is $\mathrm{span}(I_{\Lambda})$ with $K = Q(I_{\Lambda}) = \Lambda/3$, whose signature is $(1,0)$ on one side and $(0,1)$ on the other, so the completion is not standard in the sense of Fact \claimref{odd-extensions.imported-tame-walls-exceptional-algebra-residue}, and the totals $(2,3) + (0,1) = (1,4) + (1,0) = (2,4)$ match because the complement's line changes type across the wall; on the $\Lambda$ line, where the base is contractible and the wall a point, the conservation theorem reduces to the local constancy of a signature and its content is the constancy clause on $K$ alone.
\end{enumerate}

Let $(W,B)$ be as in the Setup: radical of dimension $r \geq 1$, nondegenerate part of signature $(p,q)$, $m = \dim W = p + q + r$. Then $(W,B)$ admits a completion, and:
\begin{enumerate}
\item every completion satisfies $\dim \widetilde W \geq m + r$;
\item completions of dimension exactly $m + r$ exist, and every such has signature $(p{+}r,\, q{+}r)$;
\item any two minimal completions are isometric by an isometry fixing $W$ pointwise.
\end{enumerate}
Consequently $\Cl(p,q,r)$ embeds in $\Cl(p{+}r, q{+}r)$, minimally so among the embeddings induced by completions (no completion of dimension below $m + r$ exists), and the minimal ambient algebra is unique up to an isomorphism fixing the image of $\Cl(p,q,r)$.

With $\CS = \Lambda^{\mathrm{even}} W$ as in Theorem \claimref{correspondence.fock-model-complexified-ascended-algebra}:
\begin{enumerate}
\item there is a canonical isomorphism $V_\C \cong \Lambda^2 \CS$ of $\mathrm{Spin}$-modules, under which the quadratic form corresponds to $P \mapsto \varepsilon(P \wedge P)$ for a fixed volume functional $\varepsilon$ on $\Lambda^4\CS$;
\item a vector is null exactly when its bivector is simple (decomposable), that is, represents a 2-plane of $\CS$, that is, a line of the projective spinor space $\PCS := \mathbb{P}(\CS)$ (projective twistor space);
\item consequently the completion's fibre quadric (Fact \claimref{correspondence.imported-forced-completion-fibre-quadric}), the space of null rays of $V$, double covers the real slice of the Klein quadric (a ray and its opposite give one point), whose points are the null lines of $V$ and are lines of $\PCS$.
\end{enumerate}

On the Einstein region with $\Lambda \neq 0$ of a gravitational field $(\hb, \Omega)$ on flat spacetime (Definition \claimref{obj.synthesis.gtg-flat-spacetime-gauge-fields}), whose tractor connection is the $\Cl(2,4)$ gauge field $\Omega_{T}$ of Theorem \claimref{synthesis.gtg-tractor-gauge-field-reproduces-tractor-connection}, in the notation of Lemma \claimref{dark-energy.stelle-west-vector-frozen-point}:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] \emph{(Reduction; proved.)} Parallelism of $I$ reduces the ambient $SO(2,4)$-structure to the stabiliser of $I$, the de Sitter group $SO(1,4)$ for $\Lambda > 0$ and the anti-de Sitter group $SO(2,3)$ for $\Lambda < 0$, and the tractor connection to a metric connection on the rank-five bundle $I^\perp$, of signature $(1,4)$ or $(2,3)$ respectively; the ambient curvature takes values in $\Lambda^2 I^\perp$. (The de Sitter names are used below; the anti-de Sitter branch reads identically with these substitutions, and every statement that follows uses only $\{I,V\}^\perp$, whose signature is $(1,3)$ on both branches.)
\item[(ii)] \emph{(The Stelle-West datum; proved.)} $V$ is a section of $I^\perp$ of fixed squared norm $-3/\Lambda = -\ell^2$, its stabiliser in $SO(1,4)$ (or $SO(2,3)$) is the Lorentz group of $\{I, V\}^\perp$, of signature $(1,3)$, its covariant derivative is the soldering form, the physical frame $\hb^{-1}(a)$, and the reduced connection is $D + e\wedge V/Q(V)$, on flat spacetime the gauge field $\Omega(a) - \hb^{-1}(a) \wedge V/\ell^{2}$, with Lorentz curvature block $R + \ell^{-2}\,e\wedge e$. The pair (reduced connection, $V$) is exactly the datum of Fact \claimref{dark-energy.imported-macdowell-mansouri-theorem-stelle-west}. The Stelle-West vector is therefore not the frozen point, which is parallel; it is made from the frozen point and the canonical tractor, $V = X - (3/\Lambda)\,I$.
\item[(iii)] \emph{(The action; proved, with the Stelle-West contraction of Fact \claimref{dark-energy.imported-macdowell-mansouri-theorem-stelle-west} as the imported input.)} The MacDowell-Mansouri functional of that datum is
\[
\begin{aligned}
S[\nabla, I, V] \;&\sim\; \frac{1}{Q(I)} \int \epsilon(I, V, F, F), \\
S[\nabla, I, X - \tfrac{3}{\Lambda}I] \;&\sim\; \frac{3}{\Lambda}\int \epsilon(I, X, F, F) \quad\text{(value at the canonical solution),}
\end{aligned}
\]
where $\epsilon(I, V, F, F)$ denotes the fibre volume form of $\mathcal{T}$ contracted with $I$, with $V$, and with the two bivector legs of $F\wedge F$, the first line being the functional and the second its value at the canonical Stelle-West vector of Lemma \claimref{dark-energy.stelle-west-vector-frozen-point}, written in the scale the frozen point determines (the canonical tractor $X$ carries a conformal weight): \emph{extremise the square of the curvature as seen from the point at infinity}, the frozen point supplying the reduction and the prefactor, the canonical tractor the Stelle-West slot. In the variational principle $\nabla$ ranges over the metric connections of the ambient bundle that preserve $I$, $\nabla I = 0$, equivalently those that reduce to $I^\perp$, which is the field space of Fact \claimref{dark-energy.imported-macdowell-mansouri-theorem-stelle-west} (the twenty further components per point of an unconstrained ambient connection are exactly $\nabla I$, which enter the integrand quadratically through $[I\wedge u, I\wedge v] = -Q(I)\,u\wedge v$ and would spoil the identification with the Einstein-Cartan equations); this space is carried to itself by the ambient gauge group acting on $\nabla$, $I$ and $V$ together, since $(g\nabla g^{-1})(gI) = g(\nabla I)$, and the tractor connection \claimref{dark-energy.imported-frozen-point-geometry} is the point of it occupied by the canonical solution of Lemma \claimref{dark-energy.stelle-west-vector-frozen-point}, generic gauge transformations moving it within the space without preserving the tractor shape. $I$ is a field subject to $Q(I) = \Lambda/3$, and $V$ an independent field subject to $\ip{V}{I} = 0$, $Q(V) = -3/\Lambda$, of which Lemma \claimref{dark-energy.stelle-west-vector-frozen-point} exhibits the canonical solution; the identity $Q(I)\,Q(V) = -1$ holds for either sign of $\Lambda$.
\end{enumerate}
Two consequences, each at its level:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{(Proved.)} $S$ is polynomial in the connection, $I$ and $V$, with no inverse metric anywhere: its integrand is defined wherever the ambient bundle and its connection are regular. At the Schwarzschild wall the connection is not regular (Proposition \claimref{dark-energy.frozen-point-datum-kasner-wall} on the Kasner model, transported by Fact \claimref{dark-energy.imported-weighted-limit-schwarzschild-singularity-matching}: a simple pole with the Kasner residue), and there the integrand's topological term diverges while its variational principle and field equations reach the wall as conditions on the residue; what the polynomial form yields at $\Sigma$ is the equations, not the number. the section ``What the equations vacate, and what cannot evolve'' makes this precise.
\item \emph{(Proved; the limit at the level of Fact \claimref{dark-energy.imported-macdowell-mansouri-theorem-stelle-west}.)} The prefactor $1/Q(I) = 3/\Lambda$ has a simple pole at $\Lambda = 0$ and the integrand $\epsilon(I, X, F, F)$ is polynomial in $\Lambda$: the curvature-squared presentation exists precisely when $\Lambda \neq 0$, that is, precisely when the frozen point is off the null cone. This is a boundary of the presentation, not of the action principle. By the expansion of Fact \claimref{dark-energy.imported-macdowell-mansouri-theorem-stelle-west},
\[
S \;\sim\; \frac{3}{\Lambda}\int \epsilon_{abcd}R^{ab}\wedge R^{cd} \;+\; 2\int \epsilon_{abcd}\,e^a\wedge e^b\wedge R^{cd} \;+\; \frac{\Lambda}{3}\int \epsilon_{abcd}\,e^a\wedge e^b\wedge e^c\wedge e^d ,
\]
the first term is topological with vanishing interior variation (a boundary term only), and after its subtraction the limit $\Lambda \to 0$ is the Einstein-Cartan functional $2\int\epsilon_{abcd}\,e^a\wedge e^b\wedge R^{cd}$: polynomial, defined at degenerate $e$, and no longer of curvature-squared form.
\end{enumerate}

Let $g$ be the derived metric of a gravitational field $(\hb, \Omega)$ on flat spacetime (Definition \claimref{obj.synthesis.gtg-flat-spacetime-gauge-fields}). On the invertible region: the vacuum equations are exactly the covariant constancy of the null scale tractor $
