Let $(A, V)$ denote a Clifford algebra together with its generating vector subspace $V \subset A$ (the soldering: $V$ is the image of the metric's tangent space, and $v^{2} = g(v,v)$ for $v \in V$). Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] any algebra isomorphism $(A, V) \to (A', V')$ carrying $V$ onto $V'$ preserves the signature $(p,q)$, hence the residue: the residue is an invariant of the soldered algebra;
\item[(ii)] as abstract algebras, residues $0$ and $2$, $3$ and $7$, and $4$ and $6$ are pairwise isomorphic at equal dimension, $\Cl(1,1) \cong \Cl(2,0)$, $\Cl(1,2) \cong \Cl(3,0)$, $\Cl(1,3) \cong \Cl(0,4) \cong \Mat(2, \mathbb{H})$; in particular this world's algebra and the $r = 4$ tower's are the same algebra, and no isomorphism between them carries vectors to vectors.
\end{enumerate}
So the tower charge is an invariant of the operator algebra soldered to the metric, not of the algebra: what forbids a portal in the section ``Dark, stable, counted'' is the one metric, P1.

The type is a function of the residue alone: $D = \R$ with one irreducible module at $r = 0, 2$ and two at $r = 1$; $D = \C$ with one at $r = 3, 7$; $D = \mathbb{H}$ with one at $r = 4, 6$ and two at $r = 5$. Consequently, for the P3 representatives, in the labelling convention of Proposition \claimref{dark-matter.towers-are-residues-single-clock-filter} (under the opposite one every attribution to a named member of a pair $\{r, -r\}$ passes to the other):
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] the mirror tower $r = 2$ has real (Majorana) spinors with intrinsic group $\mathbb{Z}_{2}$: no continuous charge commutes with its ground Clifford action, and in particular no $U(1)$ under which its ground spinors are charged; the fork tower $r = 0$, also real with one module, has the same intrinsic group $\mathbb{Z}_{2}$;
\item[(ii)] the residues $r = 3$ (five-dimensional) and $r = 7$ (three-dimensional) have complex spinors with intrinsic group exactly $U(1)$, a distinguished complex structure and hence exactly one intrinsic abelian charge each;
\item[(iii)] this world $r = 6$, and $r = 4, 5$, have quaternionic spinors with intrinsic group $Sp(1)$: a two-sphere of complex structures and no distinguished $U(1)$;
\item[(iv)] the odd residues $r = 1, 5$ have two inequivalent irreducible modules, distinguished by the sign of the central volume element, and $r = 3, 7$ one, the volume element acting as the complex unit.
\end{enumerate}
The intrinsic group is a lower bound on a tower's internal symmetry, not the whole of it: the rungs adjoin ladder pairs whose flags are complex structures (Remark \claimref{note.dark-matter.where-flag-defined-what-it-carries}), and the derived content of this tower's $U(1)$ is a rung-level object \cite{ButlerT12}. What the theorem fixes is what the ground algebra carries by itself.

Assume (T1) and (T2). Extend a local spanning section $k$ of $\shL$ to a covector field on a neighbourhood of $\Sig$ in $M$, and let $g_k := G(k,k)$, a smooth function vanishing on $\Sig$. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $dg_k|_\Sig$ annihilates $T\Sig$, hence is a section of the conormal bundle $N^*\Sig$;
\item $dg_k|_\Sig$ is independent of the chosen extension of $k$;
\item under $k \mapsto ck$ ($c$ a nonvanishing function), $dg_k|_\Sig$ scales by $c^2|_\Sig$.
\end{enumerate}
Consequently the assignment $k \otimes k \mapsto dg_k|_\Sig$ is a well-defined bundle map
\[
\delta \;:\; \shL \otimes \shL \;\longrightarrow\; N^*\Sig
\]
over $\Sig$, canonically attached to the degeneration. Both sides are line bundles, so $\delta$ is nowhere zero if and only if it is an isomorphism $\shL^{\otimes 2} \cong N^*\Sig$; hypothesis (T3) is exactly the demand that the degeneration itself identify the square of the dying line with the conormal line.

Let $A \in \Cl^{2}(V, Q)$ with $A^{2} = \lambda \in \R$ (true for every simple bivector $A = a \wedge b$, and for every bivector when $n \le 3$). Then $A^{2k} = \lambda^{k}$, $A^{2k+1} = \lambda^{k} A$, and $e^{A} = \sum_k A^{k}/k!$ is
\begin{equation}
e^{A} =
\begin{cases}
\cos\theta + \dfrac{\sin\theta}{\theta} A, & \lambda = -\theta^{2} < 0,\\[6pt]
\cosh\theta + \dfrac{\sinh\theta}{\theta} A, & \lambda = \theta^{2} > 0,\\[6pt]
1 + A, & \lambda = 0 .
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
In each case $e^{A} (e^{A})^{\sim} = e^{A} e^{-A} = 1$. For orthogonal $a, b$ with $Q(a) \ne 0$, $e^{\phi\, \hat{a}\hat{b}}$ (unit $\hat{a}, \hat{b}$, or $\hat{b}$ null) is the product of two invertible vectors, hence a rotor (\claimref{obj.std-algebra.rotor}): a rotation when $(a \wedge b)^{2} < 0$, a boost when $> 0$, and a null rotation or translation when $= 0$.

In $\Cl(p, q, r)$, $n = p + q + r$, with unit pseudoscalar $I$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] $I X_k = (-1)^{k(n-1)} X_k I$ for $X_k \in \Cl^{k}$; so $I$ commutes with $\Cl^{+}$, and with $\Cl^{-}$ when $n$ is odd, while it anticommutes with $\Cl^{-}$ when $n$ is even. Equivalently $I X = X I$ for $n$ odd and $I X = \hat{X} I$ for $n$ even.
\item[(ii)] $I \tilde{I} = \prod_{i} Q(e_i)$, which is $(-1)^{q}$ when $r = 0$ and $0$ when $r > 0$.
\item[(iii)] $I^{2} = (-1)^{n(n-1)/2} (-1)^{q}$ when $r = 0$ (\claimref{primer.sign}), and $I^{2} = 0$ when $r > 0$: in a degenerate algebra the pseudoscalar is nilpotent.
\end{enumerate}
